trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Prolonged sitting is a key contributor to venous stasis and worsening of post-thrombotic syndrome — consistent with chronic edema and non-healing ulcer. Domain 6 (social/occupational/exposure history) is now complete. Next, I must perform targeted physical examination (domain 7), which is essential to confirm clinical signs of chronic venous disease: varicosities, lipodermatosclerosis, atrophie blanche, hemosiderin staining, edema distribution, ulcer location/morphology (e.g., medial malleolus is classic for venous ulcers), and signs of arterial compromise (e.g., diminished pulses, hair loss, cool skin). Since I cannot examine directly, I need to request the most discriminative exam findings — starting with ulcer characteristics and surrounding skin changes.

[TEST] Please perform and report the following targeted physical examination findings:  
- Location of the ulcer (e.g., medial/lateral malleolus, anterior tibia, foot);  
- Size (length × width in cm) and depth (superficial vs. deep, presence of exposed tendon/bone);  
- Ulcer edge characteristics (e.g., irregular, undermined, well-defined);  
- Base appearance (e.g., granulating, necrotic, fibrinous, infected);  
- Presence of surrounding skin changes: varicosities, brownish discoloration (hemosiderin), induration (lipodermatosclerosis), atrophie blanche, or scaling;  
- Degree and distribution of lower-leg edema (e.g., pitting, extent from ankle upward);  
- Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulse strength (0–2+ scale).

